 4th warmest November 13 low (tied) of 82 since 1945.","t_rec":"Seattle has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Seattle's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Seattle has set <strong class=\"sig\">30 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 120 that chance alone would give, and <strong>160 record highs</strong>, more than the 120 that chance alone would give. The 2010s hold the most record highs of any decade (69). Seattle's all-time high is 108\u00b0F, on June 28, 2021.","t_chg":"Seattle has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 81 complete years: Seattle's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.52\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.42 to +0.62), about +4.2\u00b0F since 1945. Seattle's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.38\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.26 to +0.49), about +3.0\u00b0F since 1945.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 11 of 12 months. Highs rose in 8 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Seattle's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.15 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 39.3 inches a year. The wettest year was 1950 (55.14 in); the driest, 1952 (23.78 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 7 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}